beats.content:
This filter doesn't hand your callback an array. It hands it an object, a `WP_Theme_JSON_Data`, holding the theme's data. You change it with the object's `update_with()` method, then **return the object**. Here's the pattern, with a callback that makes the site's body text Twenty Twenty-Five's grey, `accent-4`:

```php
function my_first_plugin_grey_text( $theme_json ) {
	$new_data = array(
		'version' => 3,
		'styles'  => array(
			'color' => array(
				'text' => 'var:preset|color|accent-4',
			),
		),
	);

	return $theme_json->update_with( $new_data );
}
add_filter( 'wp_theme_json_data_theme', 'my_first_plugin_grey_text' );
```

`update_with()` merges your array into the theme's data and gives back the same object, so you can return its result straight away. `var:preset|color|accent-4` is theme.json's way of saying "the palette colour with the slug `accent-4`", so if the theme's grey ever changes, your text follows.

It's still a filter, so the rule from lesson 1 holds. If your callback calls `update_with()` but returns nothing, or returns an array instead of the object (even `get_data()` of it), WordPress tries to call a method on what came back, and the page stops with a fatal error.
